[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Introduction to React: Core Concepts and Principles"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"React is a JavaScript library for building user interfaces. It is maintained by Facebook and a community of individual developers and companies. React allows developers to create large web applications that can change data, without reloading the page. The primary goal of React is to provide a way to build UIs that are declarative, efficient, and flexible."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Features of React"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Declarative: React makes it painless to create interactive UIs. Design simple views for each state in your application, and React will efficiently update and render just the right components when your data changes."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Component-Based: Build encapsulated components that manage their own state, then compose them to make complex UIs. Since component logic is written in JavaScript instead of templates, you can easily pass rich data through your app and keep state out of the DOM."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Learn Once, Write Anywhere: We don’t make assumptions about the rest of your technology stack, so you can develop new features in React without rewriting existing code. React can also render on the server using Node and power mobile apps using React Native."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Components"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Components are the building blocks of any React application. They are reusable and independent pieces of code that return HTML elements to be rendered in the user interface. React has two types of components: Functional and Class components."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Functional Components"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Functional components are the simplest way to define a component. They are JavaScript functions that optionally accept props (properties) as an argument and return a React element."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"function Welcome(props) {
  return <h1>Hello, {props.name}</h1>;
}"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `Welcome` is a functional component that accepts `props` and renders a greeting with the `name` property."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Class Components"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Class components are ES6 classes that extend `React.Component`. They must define a `render()` method that returns a React element. Class components can also hold state and have lifecycle methods."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"class Welcome extends React.Component {
  render() {
    return <h1>Hello, {this.props.name}</h1>;
  }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, `Welcome` is a class component. The `render()` method returns the same greeting as the functional component, accessing the `name` property from `this.props`."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"JSX"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"JSX (JavaScript XML) is a syntax extension to JavaScript. It looks similar to HTML, but it is transformed into JavaScript function calls that create React elements. JSX makes it easier to write and understand React components."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"const element = <h1>Hello, world!</h1>;"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"This JSX code is equivalent to:"},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"const element = React.createElement(
  'h1',
  null,
  'Hello, world!'
);"},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Props"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Props (properties) are how you pass data from a parent component to a child component. Props are read-only from the perspective of the child component. They are used to customize the rendering of the child component."},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"function Welcome(props) {
  return <h1>Hello, {props.name}</h1>;
}

function App() {
  return <Welcome name="Sara" />;
}"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `App` component passes the `name` prop with the value "Sara" to the `Welcome` component."},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"State"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"State is data that is managed within a component. Unlike props, state is mutable. When the state changes, the component re-renders. State is typically used for data that changes over time, such as user input or data fetched from an API."},{"order":29,"type":"code","text":"class Counter extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { count: 0 };
  }

  increment = () => {
    this.setState({ count: this.state.count + 1 });
  }

  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        <p>Count: {this.state.count}</p>
        <button onClick={this.increment}>Increment</button>
      </div>
    );
  }
}"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `Counter` component has a `count` state variable. The `increment` method updates the state, causing the component to re-render with the new count."},{"order":31,"type":"subheading","text":"Lifecycle Methods"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"Lifecycle methods are special methods that are called at different stages of a component's life. They allow you to perform actions at specific times, such as when a component is mounted (added to the DOM), updated, or unmounted (removed from the DOM)."},{"order":33,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`componentDidMount()`: Invoked immediately after a component is mounted."},{"order":34,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`componentDidUpdate()`: Invoked immediately after an update occurs. This method is not called for the initial render."},{"order":35,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`componentWillUnmount()`: Invoked immediately before a component is unmounted and destroyed. Perform any necessary cleanup in this method, such as invalidating timers, canceling network requests, or cleaning up any DOM elements that were created."},{"order":36,"type":"code","text":"class Example extends React.Component {
  componentDidMount() {
    console.log('Component mounted!');
  }

  componentDidUpdate(prevProps, prevState) {
    console.log('Component updated!');
  }

  componentWillUnmount() {
    console.log('Component will unmount!');
  }

  render() {
    return <div>Example Component</div>;
  }
}"},{"order":37,"type":"subheading","text":"Virtual DOM"},{"order":38,"type":"paragraph","text":"React uses a virtual DOM (Document Object Model) to improve performance. The virtual DOM is a lightweight copy of the actual DOM. When the state of a component changes, React updates the virtual DOM first. Then, it compares the virtual DOM with the previous version and only updates the actual DOM with the necessary changes. This process is called reconciliation."},{"order":39,"type":"paragraph","text":"By minimizing direct manipulation of the actual DOM, React can significantly improve the performance of web applications."},{"order":40,"type":"subheading","text":"Principles of React"},{"order":41,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Single Source of Truth: Data should have a single, definitive source. This makes it easier to manage and debug your application."},{"order":42,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Unidirectional Data Flow: Data flows in one direction, from parent to child components via props. This makes it easier to reason about how data changes over time."},{"order":43,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Composition: Build complex UIs by composing smaller, reusable components."},{"order":44,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":45,"type":"paragraph","text":"React is a powerful library for building user interfaces. By understanding the core concepts and principles, you can create efficient, maintainable, and scalable web applications."}]
